WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.570
When a runtime of less than two hours was
announced for Justice League, some fans were

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.379
shocked, suspecting that the team-up movie
had been drastically cut down during reshoots

00:00:09.379 --> 00:00:10.730
and the final edit.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.550
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm breaking down and explaining

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:18.740
everything deleted and changed in the final
cut of Justice League, including some great

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:23.019
lines and scenes teased in the trailers, plus
some cameos we never got to see.

00:00:23.019 --> 00:00:27.070
I'm also currently running a giveaway for
two Justice League: The Art of The Film hardback

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:31.849
books which are full of gorgeous concept art
and behind-the-scenes shots from the set.

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:35.710
To enter, make sure you're a subscriber and
let me know what you think about any of the

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:37.870
deleted scenes or reshoots.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.680
Quick spoiler warning, I'll be discussing
changes to the movie, so be aware of that

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.350
if you haven't seen it yet.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:48.060
One reported objective of the movie's reshoots
was to lighten the tone of the film.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.070
For example Joe Morton who plays Victor Stone's
father, Silas, said he believed the studio

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.580
had requested adjustments to Cyborg to make
him less serious.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.350
There's evidence of a lot of deleted scenes
with Cyborg from the early trailers.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.210
Some of them indicate we were going to learn
more about his previous football career.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:08.750
And there's another cut scene with Cyborg
which included a cameo by Marc McClure, the

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:12.700
actor who played Jimmy Olsen in the Christopher
Reeve-era Superman movies.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.640
In the deleted Justice League clip, McClure
plays a policeman who Cyborg saves from a

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:17.640
burning wreckage.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.759
"You should probably move."

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:24.520
Ray Fisher has also revealed there was a scene
of Victor and his mother, presumably a flashback,

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.350
which Fisher described as "really special
and […] unique".

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:31.729
And there's another cut scene in the trailers
where Cyborg tells Batman he thought he wasn't

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:32.729
real.

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:35.530
"I heard about you."

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.329
"Didn't think you were real."

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:41.359
"I'm real when it's useful."

00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:45.719
In early 2016, it was also reported that Willem
Dafoe was cast as Vulko for Justice League;

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:48.600
however, his character was completely cut
from the movie.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.679
According to Jason Momoa, in an earlier version
of the film, Aquaman spent more time underwater

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:58.189
than we see in the final movie, and we would
have seen Dafoe's character Vulko as Aquaman's

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:03.009
'connection to Atlantis' and as the person
who gave Aquaman the idea that he's 'the rightful

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:04.009
king'.

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:08.160
Zack Snyder's vision for the film was to establish
Aquaman's anger at feeling like an outcast

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.929
who's not at home either at sea or on land.

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:16.120
Comic book movie fans will know Willem Dafoe
as the Green Goblin from Sam Raimi's Spider-Man

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.459
films, however, his character Vulko will be
appearing in next year's Aquaman movie.

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:25.610
The first Justice League trailer reveals deleted
scenes of some rather Snyderesque shots of

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:30.200
Bruce Wayne trekking with his horse through
the icy wilderness before he reached Aquaman's

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.420
fishing village.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:41.099
"We have to be ready.

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:48.050
You, me, the others."

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:53.540
I suspect this sort of visual storytelling
was a casualty of the editing process and

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.870
the reported directive from the Warner Bros
CEO that the final movie should be no longer

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:58.900
than two hours.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:04.319
A number of comedic moments were also victims
of this pretty strict requirement with Diana

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:09.409
and Bruce's "more more or more less" quip
getting the chop from the final film.

00:03:09.409 --> 00:03:11.670
"He said he'll fight with us?"

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:12.800
"More or less."

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:13.930
"More-more or more-less?"

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:15.469
"Probably more less."

00:03:15.469 --> 00:03:17.870
"He said no."

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.269
"He said no."

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:24.329
Another Bruce Wayne scene that ended up on
the proverbial cutting room floor showed him

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:26.960
looking at what seems to be a hologram of
Supergirl.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.840
I wonder if the idea was to have her in contact
with Batman after her cousin died.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.349
There's also some deleted dialogue in one
of the trailers, where Bruce talks about having

00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:36.349
a dream.

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:37.349
"I had a dream."

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:39.439
"It was the end of the world."

00:03:39.439 --> 00:03:43.969
And I'm guessing this was a reference to Batman's
Knightmare in BvS.

00:03:43.969 --> 00:03:49.760
Batman himself also had a short line cut when
he says "my turn" after getting into the Nightcrawler.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.980
"My turn."

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.840
Ezra Miller's Barry Allen had a stack of deleted
scenes too.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.489
First is an intriguing and very cool shot
of Barry breaking through a pane of glass

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:04.299
with the tip of his finger.

00:04:04.299 --> 00:04:08.819
The cut scene has an interesting visual parallel
to how Flash appeared to Bruce Wayne at the

00:04:08.819 --> 00:04:13.810
end of the Knightmare sequence in BvS and,
more importantly, to a moment in Justice League

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:18.620
where Barry gently pushes Wonder Woman's sword
back towards her, showing how far he's come

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:20.239
since he broke that glass.

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:24.900
Notice also that there's a woman behind Barry
in this cut moment, and I wonder whether this

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.470
could be a flashback to when either Barry
got his powers or how he learns about what

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:30.470
he can do.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:34.860
There's a cool shot of the Flash taking out
a parademon during the tunnels sequence and

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:41.500
this looks like it was cut to shorten the
action scene.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.819
Ezra Miller and Jason Momoa have also revealed
there was a comical scene cut where Barry

00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:49.850
and Aquaman are in a van and they're trying
to figure out what superhero name to give

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:52.120
him as he's not called the Flash yet.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.300
In the deleted scene, Barry is going on and
on about it, and makes a few suggestions such

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.400
as Lightning Boy or Lightning Lad.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.380
Aquaman then quips back, "how about Motor
Mouth?"

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:06.020
which makes Barry spit out his food and then
he replies, "[well what] about Comedy Man?"

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:09.990
The names Lightning Boy and Lightning Lad
are actually comic-book easter eggs as they're

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:14.550
aliases for Garth Ranzz, one of the founding
members of the Legion of Superheroes.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:18.940
By the way, while filming this van scene,
Ezra Miller had to stuff his face with burgers,

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.580
which I'm guessing would have tied in with
Barry complaining about how hungry he was

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.281
in the Batcave.

00:05:25.281 --> 00:05:30.030
Originally, we were going to get the introduction
of Iris West to the DC movie universe; however,

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.630
it looks like we won't be seeing her now until
the Flashpoint movie.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:38.069
Actress Kiersey Clemons was cast and confirmed
she filmed scenes with Zack Snyder, however,

00:05:38.069 --> 00:05:42.740
she wasn't involved in the reshoots, so it
looks she was a casualty of the edits.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:46.910
The elevator scene of the team entering the
Batcave in their superhero costumes was changed

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:49.340
to them dressed in just their regular clothes.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.389
And the Batcave scenes were also scaled back.

00:05:51.389 --> 00:05:56.030
Reports from the Batcave set in August last
year talk about numerous scenes of the Justice

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:59.780
League joking around with Alfred which we
never got to see in the movie.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:04.009
In one of those lost scenes, after seeing
the number of metahumans who've suddenly descended

00:06:04.009 --> 00:06:08.780
on the Batcave, Alfred sarcastically remarks
to Bruce Wayne that, "If you'd warned me,

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:14.030
I would have baked a cake", "I'll break open
the party hats and the piñata" and also "I'll

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:15.090
make some tea.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:16.970
Don't know where I'll find the cups."

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:21.830
Alfred did still get some nice lines in Justice
League though, like his sardonic easter-egg-laden

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:26.259
remark about "exploding wind-up penguins",
but there are also some trailer scenes with

00:06:26.259 --> 00:06:27.930
him that were chopped from the final film.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.650
"How do you know your team's strong enough?"

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:36.000
And there's one scene in particular that hints
there might have been a different return for

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.670
Superman.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:44.370
"He said you'd come."

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:56.099
"Now let's hope you're not too late."

00:06:56.099 --> 00:06:59.940
The way the film played out suggests this
might have been Superman visiting the Batcave

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.450
after he'd disappeared to his family's farmhouse
with Lois.

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:08.030
Alternatively, in this cut scene, it may be
Lois Lane who Alfred is talking to, after

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:12.199
all, Batman has asked him to 'bring in the
big gun' to help them deal with the volatile,

00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:14.409
resurrected Superman.

00:07:14.409 --> 00:07:17.660
Clark and Lois also had their scene in Smallville
cut down.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:20.080
And you can see the missing part in the trailers.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:26.240
"I'll take that as a yes?"

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:27.270
"What?"

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:29.330
"The ring."

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:33.639
We do see Lois touch her ring as she talks
to Clark in the movie, but this dialogue about

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:35.340
the ring was cut.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:40.550
A Superman moment that we didn't get to see
in the film was Superman in his black suit.

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:44.840
Now the black suit appears in a number of
comics including the Death of Superman storyline

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:46.900
in which he was killed by Doomsday.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:51.220
And many comic book fans were hoping for the
suit to make an appearance in Justice League,

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:55.879
especially after Henry Cavill posted a photo
of a section from a dark-looking Superman

00:07:55.879 --> 00:07:56.990
suit on Instagram.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:01.600
Of course, it's possible Cavill was just having
a bit of fun with fans, like Hugh Jackman

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:05.909
did when he posted a picture of a yellow-suited
Wolverine toy before Logan was released.

00:08:05.909 --> 00:08:10.599
By the way, the movie does still give a little
nod to Superman's black suit from the comics

00:08:10.599 --> 00:08:15.919
via the dark, seemingly black, trousers Superman
wears after he's resurrected, as well as in

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:19.599
the colour of the Superman banners on buildings
after his death.

00:08:19.599 --> 00:08:23.510
And despite having a shortish screentime in
Justice League, Henry Cavill was still involved

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:27.509
in the reshoots, which meant the moustache
he had for his role in Mission: Impossible

00:08:27.509 --> 00:08:29.470
had to be digitally removed in post-production.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:35.130
Wonder Woman's opening scene in London where
she saves a group of hostages from a terrorist

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:38.120
group looks like it could have played out
quite differently.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:42.039
The trailers seem to reveal the bomb going
off inside the building suggesting that, in

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:45.310
this version, she might not have saved all
the hostages.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:49.010
Which makes me wonder if this was reshot to
have a happier outcome at the beginning of

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:53.880
the film rather than something darker because,
in the actual movie, Diana throws the bomb

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:55.670
through the roof, saving everyone.

00:08:55.670 --> 00:09:00.779
I noticed a major visual change in the final
act when the Justice League go to Russia to

00:09:00.779 --> 00:09:02.350
take down Steppenwolf.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:06.330
In the first trailer, the scenes for this
part of the film all have a bluish colour,

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:09.720
whereas in later trailers this changed quite
drastically to red.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:13.030
The decision to have red skies might be a
comic-book reference to the red skies from

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:18.470
the Crisis on Infinite Earths storyline, or
perhaps a nod to the fiery, hellish landscape

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:21.330
of the planet Apokolips which Steppenwolf
hails from.

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:25.890
However, I think the red filter on the scenes
might have contributed to the CGI looking

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:29.170
less realistic in the final battle compared
to the original look.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:33.551
Speaking of Steppenwolf, the movie's big bad
has some menacing dialogue deleted from in

00:09:33.551 --> 00:09:37.060
the trailers where he references the fact
that there's nobody protecting the Earth.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:38.060
"No protectors here."

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:39.060
"No lanterns."

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:40.060
"No Kryptonian."

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:41.060
"It's World War 4."

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:50.320
And there are still a few more deleted scenes.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.220
One is a shot of the team walking into Star
Labs.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:57.339
Another is a cool shot of Wonder Woman facing
off against Steppenwolf with Aquaman and Cyborg

00:09:57.339 --> 00:09:59.510
behind her.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.490
"Shall we?"

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.750
In the final movie, we only see her alone
in this pose.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:10.100
Now, are there any deleted scenes you wish
had been included in the movie?

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:14.339
And are you happier with the shorter runtime
and lighter tone or would you have preferred

00:10:14.339 --> 00:10:15.370
something darker?

